% A-user-manual.weft — User Manual: installing, configuring and running kcc
%
% Pure prose. A practical, end-user guide to obtaining, building and using
% the compiler. No code is emitted here.

\chapter{User Manual}
\label{ch:user-manual}

This appendix is the practical guide to \emph{using} \kcc{}: how to obtain
and build it, how to compile and run a program, what every command-line
option does, how to read its diagnostics, and what it does and does not yet
support.  It assumes only that you are comfortable at a terminal; it does
not assume you have read the rest of this book.

\section{What \kcc{} Is}
\label{sec:um-what}

\kcc{} is a C compiler for the C17 language.  It takes C source files and
produces native machine code---either a relocatable object file
(\texttt{.o}) or a finished executable that you can run directly.  It is
built from scratch: it has its own preprocessor, parser, type checker,
optimizer, register allocator, and code generator, and it emits Mach-O
object code itself rather than delegating to another assembler.  Its target
is \textbf{Apple silicon}: 64-bit ARM (AArch64) running macOS.  The compiler
produced by this book, and every example in this manual, runs on that
platform.

\section{Requirements}
\label{sec:um-requirements}

To \emph{build} \kcc{} from source you need:

\begin{itemize}
\item A Mac with Apple silicon (an M-series processor) running macOS.
\item The Xcode Command Line Tools, which provide the system assembler,
      the linker \texttt{ld}, and the macOS SDK.  Install them with
      \texttt{xcode-select~-{}-install} if you have not already.
\item \texttt{weft}, the literate-programming tool that turns the
      \texttt{.weft} sources into assembly.
\item \texttt{make}, which drives the project's build recipes; it comes with
      the Xcode Command Line Tools listed above.
\item Optionally, \texttt{lualatex}, only if you want to typeset this book
      itself into a PDF.  It is not needed to build or use the compiler.
\end{itemize}

To \emph{use} the compiler once it is built you need only the Mac and the
Xcode Command Line Tools: \kcc{} invokes the system linker to produce
executables and reads the SDK to find the C runtime startup, so the Command
Line Tools must be present.

\section{Building \kcc{}}
\label{sec:um-building}

From the top of the project, a single command builds the compiler:

\begin{verbatim}
make build
\end{verbatim}

This tangles the literate sources into assembly, assembles each into an
object file, and links them into the compiler binary at
\texttt{tmp/kcc}.  That path is the compiler; everything below refers to it
as \texttt{kcc} for brevity.  To run it from anywhere, either use the full
path \texttt{tmp/kcc} or copy the binary onto your \texttt{PATH}, for
example:

\begin{verbatim}
cp tmp/kcc /usr/local/bin/kcc
\end{verbatim}

There is nothing to configure: \kcc{} has no configuration file and no
environment variables to set.  Its entire behaviour is controlled by the
command-line options described in \S\ref{sec:um-commands}.

\section{Your First Program}
\label{sec:um-first}

Create a file \texttt{hello.c}:

\begin{verbatim}
int printf(const char *fmt, ...);

int main(void) {
    printf("Hello from kcc!\n");
    return 0;
}
\end{verbatim}

Compile and link it into an executable, then run it:

\begin{verbatim}
kcc hello.c -o hello
./hello
\end{verbatim}

You should see \texttt{Hello from kcc!}.  With no \texttt{-o} option the
executable is named \texttt{a.out}, following long tradition:

\begin{verbatim}
kcc hello.c
./a.out
\end{verbatim}

That is the whole workflow for a simple program: one command to build, one
to run.

\section{Command Reference}
\label{sec:um-commands}

\kcc{} is invoked as \texttt{kcc [mode] source.c ... [options]}.  The first
argument selects what the compiler does with the source; if it is not one
of the recognised mode flags, \kcc{} performs the default action---compile
and link into an executable.  Each mode and option is listed below.

\paragraph{Modes.}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Invocation & What it does \\
\hline
\texttt{kcc a.c ... [options]}     & compile \emph{and link} into an executable (default) \\
\texttt{kcc -c a.c ... [options]}  & compile to object file(s) (\texttt{.o}), no linking \\
\texttt{kcc -E a.c}                & run only the preprocessor; print the result \\
\texttt{kcc -{}-check a.c}          & parse and type-check only; report errors, produce nothing \\
\texttt{kcc -{}-dump-tokens a.c}    & print the token stream from the lexer \\
\texttt{kcc -{}-dump-ast a.c}       & print the abstract syntax tree from the parser \\
\texttt{kcc -{}-dump-types a.c}     & print the resolved types \\
\texttt{kcc -{}-dump-ir a.c}        & print the optimized intermediate representation \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The default mode and the \texttt{-c} mode both accept several source files
at once and compile each; the default mode then links all of the resulting
objects together.  The inspection modes (\texttt{-E} and the
\texttt{-{}-dump} family, and \texttt{-{}-check}) take a single source file
and print their result to standard output, which makes them convenient to
pipe into a pager or a file.

\paragraph{Options.}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Option & Effect \\
\hline
\texttt{-o \emph{name}} & name the output; default \texttt{a.out} when linking, \\
                        & or the object name when compiling with \texttt{-c} \\
\texttt{-I\emph{dir}}   & add \emph{dir} to the \texttt{\#include} search path \\
                        & (attached \texttt{-Idir} or separated \texttt{-I dir}) \\
\texttt{-D\emph{name}}  & define \emph{name} as \texttt{1}; \texttt{-D\emph{name}=\emph{val}} \\
                        & defines it as \emph{val} (also separated \texttt{-D \emph{name}}) \\
\texttt{-Wall}          & enable the warning family (\S\ref{sec:um-warnings}) \\
\texttt{-g}             & emit DWARF debug information for a debugger \\
\texttt{-{}-emit-asm}   & build the object through textual assembly: emit a \\
                        & \texttt{.s} file and run the platform \texttt{as} on it \\
\texttt{-{}-target=x86\_64} & generate x86-64 code instead of the native ARM64 \\
                        & (currently a scalar-only subset; see \S\ref{sec:um-x86}) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Options may appear in any order relative to the source files.  A word that
is not an option and not a mode flag is treated as a source file.

\texttt{-{}-emit-asm} selects \kcc{}'s second code-emission strategy.  The
default back end encodes each instruction and writes the Mach-O object
directly; with \texttt{-{}-emit-asm} \kcc{} instead renders the generated code
as assembly text (\texttt{.s}) and invokes the system assembler to produce the
object.  The two produce equivalent objects for ordinary code, but the textual
path is what makes \emph{inline assembly} (\S\ref{sec:um-inline-asm}) possible,
because the direct encoder cannot assemble an arbitrary \texttt{asm} template.
It works in both the default (link) and \texttt{-c} modes.

\section{Compiling and Linking in Separate Steps}
\label{sec:um-separate}

For a project of more than one file you can compile each translation unit to
an object and link them later, exactly as with any C compiler:

\begin{verbatim}
kcc -c util.c   -o util.o
kcc -c main.c   -o main.o
kcc util.o main.o -o program
./program
\end{verbatim}

Or you can let \kcc{} do it all in one step by naming every source on the
command line:

\begin{verbatim}
kcc util.c main.c -o program
\end{verbatim}

\section{Warnings}
\label{sec:um-warnings}

By default \kcc{} reports only errors---conditions that make a program
ill-formed.  Passing \texttt{-Wall} additionally enables a family of
warnings for suspicious but legal code: calling a function with no visible
declaration, a local variable that is never used, a non-\texttt{void}
function that can fall off its end without returning, a comparison that
mixes signed and unsigned operands, and an implicit conversion between an
integer and a pointer.  Warnings do not stop compilation; they are advice.

\section{Debug Information}
\label{sec:um-debug}

The \texttt{-g} option makes \kcc{} emit DWARF debugging information
alongside the code, so that a debugger can map machine addresses back to
functions and source lines.  The information is function-granular: it
records where each function begins, which is enough for a backtrace to name
the right functions.

\section{Inline Assembly}
\label{sec:um-inline-asm}

\kcc{} accepts basic GNU inline assembly---\texttt{asm("\emph{template}");}
(also spelled \texttt{\_\_asm\_\_}, and with an ignored \texttt{volatile},
\texttt{inline}, or \texttt{goto} qualifier)---where \emph{template} is a
string literal containing one or more assembler instructions.  Because the
default back end encodes only the fixed instruction set \kcc{} itself selects,
inline assembly requires the textual back end: compile with
\texttt{-{}-emit-asm} (\S\ref{sec:um-commands}), which renders the code as
\texttt{.s} and lets the system assembler assemble the template verbatim.

\begin{verbatim}
int main(void) {
    asm("mov x16, #1");   /* SYS_exit */
    asm("mov x0, #7");
    asm("svc #0x80");     /* exit(7) */
    return 0;
}
\end{verbatim}

Extended assembly with operand constraints is supported in a common subset:
register outputs (\texttt{=r}), inputs (\texttt{r}), read-write operands
(\texttt{+r}), and integer immediates (\texttt{i}), with up to two operands and
\texttt{\%0}/\texttt{\%1} (or \texttt{\%w0}/\texttt{\%x0}) placeholders.  A
\texttt{"memory"} or \texttt{"cc"} clobber is accepted; the compiler treats the
asm as a barrier so cached values are re-read afterwards.

\begin{verbatim}
int y = 5, x;
asm("mov %0, %1" : "=r"(x) : "r"(y));   /* x = y  */
asm("add %0, %0, #1" : "+r"(x));        /* x += 1 */
\end{verbatim}

Forms outside this subset---three or more operands, memory (\texttt{m}) or
specific-register constraints, and \texttt{asm goto}---are rejected with a
diagnostic rather than miscompiled.

\section{Generating x86-64 Code}
\label{sec:um-x86}

\kcc{}'s native output is ARM64 for Apple silicon, but it can also generate
x86-64 code with the \texttt{-{}-target=x86\_64} option.  This selects a second
back end that renders the program as x86-64 assembly, assembles it with the
system \texttt{as -arch x86\_64}, and links a Mach-O executable that runs on
Apple silicon under the Rosetta translator:

\begin{verbatim}
kcc --target=x86_64 add.c -o add
./add                 # runs under Rosetta; echo $? shows the exit status
\end{verbatim}

The x86-64 back end is being built up one capability at a time and today
covers the \emph{scalar} language: integer and pointer arithmetic, the full
set of operators and comparisons, control flow (\texttt{if}, \texttt{while},
\texttt{for}, \texttt{?:}, \texttt{\&\&}/\texttt{||}), local variables and
arrays, structs addressed by member, mutable file-scope global variables,
string literals, and function calls following the System~V AMD64 calling
convention---including recursion, calls that pass arguments on the stack, and
calls to library functions such as \texttt{printf}.  A whole program such as

\begin{verbatim}
int printf(const char *, ...);
int count = 0;
int main(void) {
    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
        if (i % 2 == 0) { printf("%d even\n", i); count++; }
    printf("evens=%d\n", count);
    return count;
}
\end{verbatim}

\noindent compiles, links, and runs in one step with output identical to a
mainstream x86-64 compiler.

\paragraph{Targeting Linux (ELF).}  The same instruction selection can emit
x86-64 assembly in the Linux/ELF dialect with \texttt{-{}-target=x86\_64-linux}:
symbols are un-prefixed, read-only data goes in \texttt{.rodata}, zeroed globals
use \texttt{.comm}, and an external reference resolves directly (for a static
link) rather than through the Mach-O GOT.  Because \kcc{} runs on macOS---whose
system assembler emits only Mach-O---this mode \emph{writes the assembly to the
output path and stops} rather than assembling it; you finish the build on a
Linux host (or in a container):

\begin{verbatim}
kcc --target=x86_64-linux -c prog.c -o prog.s   # emits ELF-dialect asm
# then, on a Linux/x86-64 host:
as -o prog.o prog.s  &&  ld -o prog prog.o ...   # assemble + link + run
\end{verbatim}

What the x86-64 back end does \emph{not} yet handle is a \texttt{const} global
whose value lives in read-only data (a reference to one becomes an unresolved
symbol at link time---a clean link error, never a wrong value), a
block-scoped \texttt{static} local, floating-point arithmetic, or passing a
struct by value.  These are planned; until then, keep such a program on the
default ARM64 path, which supports the whole language.  The native path is
entirely unaffected by this option---omitting \texttt{-{}-target} (or writing
\texttt{-{}-target=arm64}) compiles for ARM64 exactly as before.

\section{Reading Diagnostics}
\label{sec:um-diagnostics}

When \kcc{} rejects a program it prints a diagnostic that names the file,
the line and column, the nature of the problem, and---like a modern
compiler---a caret pointing at the offending token in a copy of the source
line.  It does not stop at the first error: it recovers and continues so
that a single run reports as many independent problems as it can find, which
saves the edit-compile cycle of fixing them one at a time.  A run that
prints diagnostics and produces no output has failed; a run that prints
nothing (or only warnings, under \texttt{-Wall}) and produces the requested
file has succeeded.

\section{Looking Inside the Compiler}
\label{sec:um-inspect}

The inspection modes are there for the curious and for anyone debugging a
surprising result.  \texttt{-E} shows exactly what the preprocessor
produced---useful when a macro does not expand as expected.
\texttt{-{}-dump-ast} shows how the parser understood the program's
structure.  \texttt{-{}-dump-ir} shows the optimized intermediate
representation, the form on which the optimizer and code generator work; it
is the clearest window on what code \kcc{} will actually generate, and on
what the optimizer did or did not simplify.  None of these modes produces an
object file; they print and exit.

\section{What \kcc{} Supports}
\label{sec:um-scope}

\kcc{} implements the C17 language broadly: the full expression and
statement grammar, the complete type system with structs, unions, enums,
bit-fields and function pointers, the preprocessor with macros and
conditional inclusion, and the harder corners of the standard such as
designated initializers, \texttt{\_Generic} selection, \texttt{\_Bool}
normalization, \texttt{volatile} semantics, and correctly typed integer
constants.  It provides a small set of freestanding standard headers so that
ordinary programs using \texttt{printf} and friends compile without an
external installation.  It optimizes---constant folding, dead-code
elimination, common-subexpression elimination, and keeping locals in
registers---and allocates both integer and floating values to registers.

Its current limits are worth stating plainly so you are not surprised.  Its
native, full-language target is Apple silicon (ARM64); a second, x86-64 target
is under construction and today compiles the scalar subset described in
\S\ref{sec:um-x86} (the framework behind it is Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting}).
One tail corner of C17 remains:
propagating \texttt{volatile} from a qualified \emph{object} through member
access (a member or bit-field declared \texttt{volatile} is already
honoured).  Nested designator chains, brace elision, and per-operation
single-precision rounding for \texttt{float} arithmetic are now fully
supported.  Appendix~\ref{ch:roadmap} tracks the state of every feature in
detail.

\section{Building This Book}
\label{sec:um-docs}

If you have \texttt{lualatex} installed and want the typeset PDF of this
literate program, run:

\begin{verbatim}
make weave
\end{verbatim}

This is purely optional and unrelated to using the compiler.

\section{For Contributors}
\label{sec:um-contributors}

The project's \texttt{make} recipes cover the full development loop.  The
most useful are \texttt{make build} (produce the compiler), \texttt{make
test} (build and run the unit tests), and the conformance suites that guard
against regressions: \texttt{make test-codegen} (end-to-end code-generation
tests), \texttt{make difftest} (compile a corpus with both \kcc{} and the
reference compiler and compare their output), \texttt{make selfhost}
(compile a piece of \kcc{}'s own C with \kcc{} and check the result),
\texttt{make diagtest} and \texttt{make warntest} (check that invalid
programs are rejected and that warnings fire as expected), \texttt{make
interop} (validate the calling convention against the reference compiler),
and \texttt{make e2e} (the one-step compile-and-run path).  All changes go
through the \texttt{.weft} sources; the tangled assembly under \texttt{tmp}
is generated and must never be edited by hand.

\section{Troubleshooting}
\label{sec:um-troubleshooting}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Linking fails, or the executable will not run.}  \kcc{} calls
      the system linker and needs the macOS SDK.  Confirm the Xcode Command
      Line Tools are installed (\texttt{xcode-select~-p} should print a
      path) and that \texttt{xcrun~-{}-show-sdk-path} prints a valid SDK
      location.
\item \textbf{A program compiles but behaves unexpectedly.}  Use
      \texttt{-{}-dump-ir} to see the code \kcc{} generated, and check the
      feature against the limits in \S\ref{sec:um-scope}; a divergence from
      another compiler on a corner feature is more likely a known gap than a
      miscompilation of ordinary code, which the difference suite guards
      closely.
\item \textbf{A macro does not expand as you expect.}  Run \texttt{kcc -E}
      on the file to see the preprocessed text.
\item \textbf{\texttt{kcc} is not found.}  The built compiler lives at
      \texttt{tmp/kcc}; invoke it by that path, or copy it onto your
      \texttt{PATH} as shown in \S\ref{sec:um-building}.
\end{itemize}
